      "content": "# Transaction Isolation, Race Conditions, and Deadlock Prevention\n\n## 1. Transaction Isolation\n\nTransaction isolation defines the degree to which the operations of one transaction are separated from those of other concurrent transactions. The SQL standard defines four isolation levels, each offering a different balance between consistency and concurrency.\n\n### The Four Isolation Levels\n\n| Level | Name | Description |\n|-------|------|-------------|\n| 1 | **Read Uncommitted** | A transaction can read data that has been modified but not yet committed by another transaction. |\n| 2 | **Read Committed** | A transaction can only read data that has been committed. Prevents *dirty reads*. |\n| 3 | **Repeatable Read** | A transaction sees a consistent snapshot of data as it existed at the start of the transaction. Prevents *non-repeatable reads*. |\n| 4 | **Serializable** | Transactions are executed as if they ran sequentially, one after another. Prevents *phantom reads*. |\n\n### Example: Bank Transfer\n\nConsider two transactions transferring money between accounts A and B.\n\n```sql\n-- Transaction T1: Transfer $100 from A to B\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance -",
